<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Northern Liang</h1><p class="st-meta">397 CE – 460 CE · capital Zhangye, Guzang, Gaochang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Hexi Corridor kingdom of the Juqu family, a major centre of early Chinese Buddhism, whose survivors carried the state to Turpan after 439.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 397 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-mengxun">Juqu Mengxun</a>, a Lushuihu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> chief, rebelled against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-liang-386-403">Later Liang</a> and set up the Chinese official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duan-ye">Duan Ye</a> as ruler at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> commandery near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhangye">Zhangye</a>. In 401 he killed Duan Ye and took power himself, later moving the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Guzang</a> (Wuwei) in 412 and conquering <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-liang">Western Liang</a> in 421.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-liang-shidu">Liang</a> was a prosperous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-mengxun">Juqu Mengxun</a> was a devout Buddhist who sponsored the translator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmakshema">Dharmakshema</a>, whose version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahaparinirvana-sutra">Mahaparinirvana Sutra</a> became influential, and cave temples such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiantishan-grottoes">Tiantishan</a>. Scholars from Liang later carried Hexi learning to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-wei">Northern Wei</a> court.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-wei">Northern Wei</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Guzang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-mujian">Juqu Mujian</a> in 439, completing its unification of the north. His brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-wuhui">Juqu Wuhui</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-anzhou">Juqu Anzhou</a> fled west and ruled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gaochang-kingdom">Gaochang</a> from 442 until the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate</a> killed Anzhou in 460.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/northern-liang.jpg" alt="Northern Liang" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixteen_Kingdoms_436_AD_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>397 CE – 460 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zhangye, Guzang, Gaochang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Duan Ye, Juqu Mengxun, Juqu Mujian</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Liang (Juqu clan)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=428&amp;focus=northern-liang" aria-label="Northern Liang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7169 781 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Northern Liang lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7489 1037L8059 884M7489 1037L7329 998M7489 1037L7771 1192M7489 1037L7402 767"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8059 884h0M7329 998h0M7771 1192h0M7402 767h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7489 1037h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7489 1037h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Zhangye. <a href="/atlas?year=428&amp;focus=northern-liang">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
